WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.980
Profit targets, your monthly profit and loss, and cash flow.

2
00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:08.830
This is the video for Module 3 of Pricing, Costing and Profit Planning.

3
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:22.200
Break-even tells you how to stop losing money. A profit target tells you how to make the money you want. Just add the profit you want to your fixed costs, then divide by your contribution per unit.

4
00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:29.700
Ngozi's fixed costs are 110,000 naira a month. Her own wage is already inside them.

5
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:35.810
She wants 100,000 naira profit on top, to save for a bigger fryer.

6
00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:48.580
Together that is 210,000 naira. Divided by 760, that is 276.3. Round up: 277 packs a month.

7
00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:54.800
That becomes 70 packs a week, or 11 packs a day. A number she can tick off in her notebook.

8
00:00:56.050 --> 00:01:03.730
A target is only useful if you can reach it. Can you make it? Ngozi can fry 6 to 8 batches a month, so yes.

9
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:14.090
Can you sell it? She sold about 250 packs last month, so 277 is a stretch of about 11 percent, not a fantasy.

10
00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:20.710
Can you fund it? Each batch costs 47,000 naira before any money comes back.

11
00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:38.220
Then choose your lever: raise the price, cut variable cost, cut fixed cost, or sell more. At 1,500 naira, Ngozi would need 375 packs for the same profit. Low prices demand far more work and cash.

12
00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:47.380
A profit and loss statement is your monthly scorecard. Start with sales: everything you sold, including sales on credit.

13
00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:54.350
Subtract the cost of goods sold. That is the variable cost of the units you actually sold.

14
00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.680
That gives gross profit. Units still on your shelf are stock, not this month's cost.

15
00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:07.730
Subtract your operating expenses: rent, your wage, data, levies and equipment wear.

16
00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:15.420
What is left is your net profit, or net loss. With good daily records, this takes about 30 minutes.

17
00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:29.940
Here is a real month. Ngozi made 350 packs. She sold 260 at 1,700 naira and 60 at 1,400 naira, and 30 were still in stock.

18
00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:34.690
Her total sales were 526,000 naira.

19
00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:48.680
320 packs sold, at 940 naira each, is 300,800 naira cost of goods sold. Her gross profit is 225,200 naira.

20
00:02:49.930 --> 00:03:02.150
After 110,000 naira of operating expenses, her net profit is 115,200 naira. That beats her target by 15,200 naira.

21
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:10.480
Watch your gross margin and net margin each month. Hers are about 42.8 percent and 21.9 percent.

22
00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:21.500
Cash flow is the actual movement of money in and out. Many small businesses fail not because they are unprofitable, but because they run out of cash.

23
00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:26.630
Credit sales count as sales, but no cash arrives until the customer pays.

24
00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:32.850
Unsold stock was paid for in cash, but is not yet a cost in the P&L.

25
00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:38.470
And loan repayments take cash out, without being an expense in the P&L.

26
00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:45.160
Ngozi made 115,200 naira profit. So where is her cash?

27
00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:56.080
Only her retail sales, 442,000 naira, came in. The supermarket's 84,000 naira is not due until next month.

28
00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:03.730
Out went 466,000 naira for 7 batches, fixed costs and the loan repayment.

29
00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:17.050
Her cash fell to 16,000 naira, not enough for even one 47,000 naira batch. That is why you forecast cash week by week, and act early on any week that ends negative.

30
00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:30.760
Take a deposit, for example 50 percent, before starting custom orders. Give credit only to known customers, in writing, with a due date. Keep a buffer of at least one month of fixed costs.

31
00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:37.490
Do not borrow to cover a loss. And be wary of investment offers promising high monthly returns.

32
00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:55.210
To remember. Add your profit target to fixed costs and divide by contribution. Turn it into a daily number. Build your P&L every month. Remember that profit is not cash. And forecast cash weekly, so you act before a week goes negative.

33
00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:04.860
Now set your own profit target with daily and weekly numbers, prepare last month's profit and loss, and build a four-week cash forecast.

34
00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:07.150
Continue with the lessons below.
